---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to switch from service company to product company: the real roadmap';
const description =
  'How to switch from service company to product company: the DSA and system design gap, resume rewrite, timing by experience level, and a proof plan that gets you shortlisted.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-change/how-to-switch-from-service-company-to-product-company';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-05';
const publishedDateLabel = '5 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'why-hard', label: 'Why the switch is genuinely hard' },
  { id: 'gap', label: 'The real skill gap, not the resume gap' },
  { id: 'timing', label: 'When to switch by experience level' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this move' },
  { id: 'prep-plan', label: 'The preparation plan that actually works' },
  { id: 'resume', label: 'Fixing the resume so it survives the first filter' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you start applying' },
  { id: 'non-coding', label: 'If you are not a pure developer' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes that quietly kill the switch' },
  { id: 'offer-notice', label: 'Negotiating the offer and serving notice' },
  { id: 'gcc-alternative', label: 'The GCC route: a real third option' },
  { id: 'stay-or-go', label: 'When staying is the smarter move' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const salaryRows = [
  {
    tier: 'Service company, 0-2 years',
    range: 'Rs 3.5-7 LPA',
    note: 'Stable entry point with structured training, but increments are slow and largely tenure-based, not skill-based.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'Product company / funded startup, 0-2 years',
    range: 'Rs 8-15 LPA',
    note: 'Same experience band, but selection is far more skill-filtered through DSA and system design rounds.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'Service to product switch, 2-5 years',
    range: '30-80% pay jump',
    note: 'The most common range reported for engineers who prepare seriously before switching, not for a random application.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'GCC (Global Capability Centre) roles, 4-10 years',
    range: 'Often product-comparable',
    note: 'Technology and BFSI together made up 56% of all GCC hiring in 2026, with most demand concentrated in the 4-10 year experience band.',
  },
  {
    tier: 'Senior service-company track (architect, delivery manager)',
    range: 'Rs 25-35 LPA',
    note: 'A real, valid path if you like ownership over delivery and client relationships more than product-metric-driven work.',
  },
];

const gapRows = [
  {
    area: 'Depth of problem-solving',
    service: 'Ticket-based work: fix, patch, deploy inside a defined scope set by a client.',
    product: 'Own a feature end to end, argue for trade-offs, defend a design choice under review.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Data structures & algorithms',
    service: 'Used lightly if at all; rarely tested day to day on the job.',
    product: 'Tested directly in interviews; 2-4 rounds is normal even for a mid-level hire.',
  },
  {
    area: 'System design',
    service: 'Usually absent from daily work below architect level.',
    product: 'Expected from 2-3 years experience onward; even freshers may face basic low-level design questions.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Ownership signal on a resume',
    service: '"Worked on client project for banking domain" reads as vague scope.',
    product: '"Built a service handling X requests/second, cut latency by Y%" reads as measurable ownership.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Code review and engineering practice',
    service: 'Client SLAs and process compliance often matter more than code elegance.',
    product: 'Strong review culture, testing discipline, and CI/CD exposure are assumed baseline.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology',
    body: 'Product-company interview loops run 4-7 rounds across DSA, system design, and behavioral fit, often stacked into intense single-day loops. Can you hold focus and composure across a long, high-pressure interview day, then do it again for the next company two weeks later?',
    note: 'If a single hard interview wrecks your week, build interview stamina deliberately before you apply broadly, not after a string of rejections.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'A serious switch usually needs 3-6 months of consistent DSA and system-design preparation on top of a full-time job. Can your current schedule, energy, and family situation actually support that for the length it takes, or do you need a slower, staged plan instead?',
    note: 'A rushed 3-week prep sprint before spray-applying to fifty companies is the single most common way this move fails.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'The demand is real: India\'s GCCs alone are projected to cross 5 lakh new jobs in 2026, and product-focused hiring keeps growing even while some services firms trim headcount. But almost two-thirds of new GCC roles now require AI, data, or automation skill on top of core engineering.',
    note: 'Demand existing and demand being easy to access are two different facts. Plan for the harder one.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'AI is already compressing the routine, ticket-based work that dominates a lot of service-company day-to-day. The safer position is becoming someone who can design, verify, and own a system, not someone whose entire value is following a spec exactly as written.',
    note: 'This is exactly the layer product companies pay more for, and exactly the layer AI cannot fully replace yet.',
  },
];

const gateItems = [
  {
    num: 'Gate 1',
    title: 'Proof of skill',
    body: 'Solve 150-200 DSA problems across the 15-20 core patterns (not 500 random ones), and ship one deployed side project or a real open-source contribution that is not a tutorial clone. If 70%+ of it is your own thinking, it counts.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 2',
    title: 'Proof of communication',
    body: 'Rewrite two resume bullets from your current service-company work into outcome language: what you built, what changed because of it, and what number moved. If you cannot quantify it, you have not finished this gate yet.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 3',
    title: 'Proof of value',
    body: 'Get one honest opinion from someone already inside a product company or GCC (LinkedIn message, referral call, or a mock interview). Ask directly: "would this resume and this project get me shortlisted at your company?" Use their answer, not your own hope, to decide if you are ready to apply widely.',
  },
];

const timingRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Fresher, under 6 months in',
    reality: 'The easiest window to move. Recruiters read a fresher\'s resume as still forming, so a strong DSA score and one solid project can outweigh a short service-company tenure almost entirely.',
  },
  {
    stage: '6 months to 1.5 years',
    reality: 'Still a favourable window. Keep the current job as income and stability while you prepare; do not quit first and prepare after, since runway pressure makes interview performance worse, not better.',
  },
  {
    stage: '1.5 to 4 years',
    reality: 'The hardest stretch. Without a strong hands-on project or a visible ownership story, this band is where recruiters most often assume the experience carries limited transferable weight. This is exactly where proof of work has to do the talking the resume cannot.',
  },
  {
    stage: '4-10 years',
    reality: 'A different door opens here: GCCs and larger product organisations actively hire in this band for specialist and lead roles, especially with a domain, cloud, or AI-adjacent skill layered on top of the core stack.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Preparing for 3 weeks, then applying to 50 companies at once',
    body: 'A rushed prep sprint produces a string of first-round rejections that damages confidence faster than it builds skill. A steadier stretch of consistent daily practice, run for however long it genuinely takes you, beats a frantic short burst almost every time.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Solving 500 random LeetCode problems instead of 150-200 pattern-mapped ones',
    body: 'Breadth without pattern recognition means you can solve a problem you have seen before and freeze on a new variation of the same pattern. Two to three problems a day, tied to a specific pattern, beats ten unrelated ones crammed into a weekend.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Leaving the resume in "client project" language',
    body: '"Worked on a banking domain project at a service company" tells a recruiter almost nothing about what you actually built or changed. Rewrite every bullet around a concrete action, a system, and a measurable outcome, even if the number is an estimate you can defend in the interview.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Ignoring system design until "later"',
    body: 'System design questions now show up even in some fresher and early-career product-company interviews at a basic, low-level-design depth. Waiting until you have 3 years of experience to start learning it means you walk into your first real shot underprepared.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Assuming every product-company employee started at a product company',
    body: 'A large share of engineers at Flipkart, Swiggy, Razorpay, and similar companies started exactly where you are now. The switch is common enough that it is a known, well-mapped path, not a rare exception you are attempting alone.',
  },
];

const nonCodingItems = [
  {
    label: 'Real path',
    title: 'QA and testing toward automation and product QA',
    body: 'Manual testing experience is a real foundation. The multiplier is automation scripting, API testing tools, and one documented case where you caught or prevented a real production issue. Product companies pay more for testers who can reason about risk, not just execute test cases.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Real path',
    title: 'Technical support toward platform or reliability work',
    body: 'Ticket handling, escalation management, and incident response are underrated proof of production reality. Package a real incident you resolved as a short case note: what broke, what you checked, what fixed it, and what you would change next time.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Real path',
    title: 'Business analysis toward product analytics or product ownership',
    body: 'Requirement-gathering and stakeholder communication translate directly into early product-management or business-analyst-at-a-product-company roles. Add one dashboard, one metrics-backed recommendation, or one specification document you can show without breaking confidentiality.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How to switch from service company to product company as a fresher?',
    a: 'Start immediately rather than waiting. Freshers get read as "still forming," so a strong DSA foundation (150-200 problems across core patterns) and one real deployed project can outweigh a short service-company tenure. Apply through referrals and LinkedIn outreach, and do not wait for a "perfect" resume before you start interviewing, since interview reps themselves are part of the preparation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many years of experience make it hardest to switch from service to product?',
    a: 'The 1.5 to 4 year band is commonly described as the hardest window, because recruiters often assume the experience carries limited transferable depth unless you can show a specific hands-on project or ownership story. Under 1.5 years and over 4 years both tend to have clearer, more established paths in, whether through fresher-friendly hiring or GCC and specialist-level demand.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the realistic salary jump when switching from service to product company in India?',
    a: 'Most reported switches land in the 30-80% pay-increase range when the candidate has prepared seriously. Entry-level service-company pay commonly runs Rs 3.5-7 LPA, while product companies and well-funded startups pay roughly Rs 8-15 LPA for comparable experience. The gap widens further for engineers who add a cloud, AI, or system-design skill layer on top of core fundamentals.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to quit my service-company job before preparing for product-company interviews?',
    a: 'No, and doing so usually hurts more than it helps. Keep the income and stability while you prepare, since running out of runway mid-preparation adds pressure that measurably worsens interview performance. Most successful switches happen while still employed, using early mornings, evenings, or weekends for a structured prep stretch.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I am a tester, support engineer, or business analyst, not a pure developer?',
    a: 'You still have a real path, just a different one than core development. Testers can move toward automation and product QA, support engineers toward platform or reliability roles, and business analysts toward product analytics or associate product-management roles. The core requirement is the same across all three: package your production-reality experience into a specific, measurable proof asset instead of a vague job-title description.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are GCCs (Global Capability Centres) a good alternative to a pure product company?',
    a: 'Yes, and often an underrated one. India\'s GCCs are projected to cross roughly 5 lakh new jobs in 2026, with strong demand concentrated in the 4-10 year experience band and nearly two-thirds of new roles requiring AI, data, or automation skill. Many GCCs offer product-company-comparable pay, ownership, and engineering culture, sometimes with an easier entry path than a pure product company for candidates already inside a services or captive setup.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does the whole switch usually take from decision to offer?',
    a: 'Most people who prepare seriously report a 3-6 month stretch from starting structured DSA and system-design practice to receiving an offer, though this varies with how much time you can realistically give it each week and how strong your starting point is. Treat it as a stretch of consistent effort rather than a fixed deadline, since rushing the preparation to hit an arbitrary date is one of the most common reasons the first attempt fails.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-change"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>How to switch from service company to product company comes down to closing one specific
    gap: depth.</strong>
    Service-company work builds breadth and adaptability across many client projects. Product companies
    hire for depth: strong data structures and algorithms, system design thinking, and visible ownership
    of something you actually built and can defend. Most engineers who make this move successfully report
    a 30-80% pay jump, but the number is really a side effect. The real prize is a job where your daily
    work compounds into a genuine <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> instead of another year of
    ticket-based work that looks the same on your resume as it did last year — and a skill portfolio that
    actually compounds is what unlocks stronger income opportunities and moves you toward earlier
    financial freedom, not the company logo on your ID card.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The switch is about depth, not location: DSA, system design, and one real deployed project close most of the gap that keeps service-company resumes from getting shortlisted.</li>
      <li>Most engineers who prepare seriously report a 30-80% pay jump; entry-level service pay commonly runs Rs 3.5-7 LPA versus roughly Rs 8-15 LPA at product companies for comparable experience.</li>
      <li>The 1.5-4 year experience band is the hardest window to switch from; freshers and 4-10 year professionals (especially toward GCCs) generally have clearer paths in.</li>
      <li>A rushed 3-week prep sprint before spray-applying everywhere is the single most common way this attempt fails. A steady, honest stretch of consistent practice works better than a frantic burst.</li>
      <li><strong>The real goal is not the company logo. It is building a genuine skill portfolio and visible proof of work, because that is what actually unlocks stronger income opportunities and earlier financial freedom, not the badge on your ID card.</strong></li>
      <li>Testers, support engineers, and business analysts have real, mapped paths in too, not just core developers.</li>
      <li>Use service-company domain and client context as an edge, then add AI-assisted engineering, product judgment, communication, and shipped proof. That portfolio can later support specialist, consulting, freelance, or product ownership paths too.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still deciding whether software engineering itself is the right long-term bet, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-software-engineering-a-good-career-in-india/">is software engineering
    a good career in India</a> first for the wider picture.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a clearer, honest read on your current skill gaps before you commit months of prep time,
    use the <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career & Skills Compass</a> to shortlist what to build first.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "how to switch from service company to product company"</h2>

  <p>
    Close the depth gap, then apply with proof, not with hope.
  </p>

  <p>
    Product companies are not rejecting you because you worked at a service company. They are rejecting
    resumes and interview performances that do not yet show the specific things they hire for: strong
    problem-solving under pressure, system design thinking, and a track record of owning something end to
    end instead of executing a defined ticket.
  </p>

  <p>
    Close that gap deliberately, and the "service company" label on your resume stops being the obstacle
    people assume it is.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not a "just apply more" problem, and it is not a "you are permanently behind" problem either.
      It is a specific, learnable skill gap with a known shape. The engineers who close it in a few focused
      months usually get through. The ones who apply to fifty companies with an unchanged resume and no new
      preparation usually do not, no matter how many times they try.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-hard">Why the switch is genuinely harder than it looks from outside</h2>

  <p>
    Two things make this switch harder than a normal job change, and neither of them is really about you.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What actually makes this hard</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Service companies are structured to build breadth and client adaptability across many projects, not deep ownership of one system over years.</li>
      <li>Product-company interview loops commonly run 4-7 rounds across DSA, system design, and behavioral fit, versus a simpler 2-3 round process typical at many service companies.</li>
      <li>A resume line like "worked on client project for banking domain" reads as vague scope next to "built a service handling X requests per second," even when the underlying work quality was similar.</li>
      <li>India produces a large, steady supply of engineering graduates every year, so product companies can afford to filter hard on depth signals rather than potential alone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of this means the work you did at your service company was low-value. It means the market
      cannot see that value from the resume alone, so you have to translate it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gap">The real skill gap, not the resume gap</h2>

  <p>
    Fix the resume last, not first. The resume only becomes convincing once the underlying gap is actually
    closed.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What matters</th>
          <th>Typical service-company reality</th>
          <th>What product companies expect</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {gapRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What matters"><strong>{item.area}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Typical service-company reality">{item.service}</td>
            <td data-label="What product companies expect">{item.product}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Working on a system used by real users, with senior code review, testing discipline, and design
      trade-off conversations, genuinely builds a different muscle than delivering a client-scoped feature
      on a deadline. That gap is real. It is also completely closable with deliberate, structured practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is also the part that compounds: closing this gap is what turns a job change into a genuine
      <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>, not just a new logo on your resume — and a skill
      portfolio you can actually prove is what unlocks stronger income opportunities and earlier financial
      freedom over the next decade, not one negotiated CTC number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="timing">When to switch, by experience level</h2>

  <p>
    Timing changes the strategy more than most guides admit. What works at 6 months does not work the same
    way at 3 years.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Experience stage</th>
          <th>What is actually true for you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {timingRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Experience stage"><strong>{item.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What is actually true for you">{item.reality}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      If you are in the 1.5-4 year band and reading this feeling discouraged, the fix is not to panic and
      apply faster. It is to build one strong, specific hands-on project or a documented ownership story
      that gives a recruiter a concrete reason to look past the tenure length on the resume.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit months to this switch</h2>

  <p>
    A single salary comparison cannot tell you whether this switch fits your actual life right now. The
    4-Checkpoint Protocol narrows the decision to what genuinely matters for your situation.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="prep-plan">The preparation plan that actually works</h2>

  <p>
    Most people over-index on volume and under-index on pattern recognition and consistency. Both matter,
    but in a different order than most forum threads suggest.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-numbered-list">
    <li>
      <span class="rnl-num">1</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Map the 15-20 core DSA patterns first</strong>, then solve 2-3 problems a day tied to a
        specific pattern, instead of grinding 10 random problems in one sitting. Aim for roughly 150-200
        well-chosen problems rather than 500 unrelated ones. Consistency across a stretch of time, run for
        however long it genuinely takes you, beats a single intense week before an interview.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="rnl-num">2</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Add basic system design early</strong>, not after you feel "ready." Even some fresher and
        early-career product-company interviews now include low-level design questions, so waiting until
        year three to start means walking into your first real shot underprepared.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="rnl-num">3</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Ship one real project or open-source contribution</strong> that mirrors a production
        problem, not a tutorial clone. A working, deployed, documented project with a clear README
        explaining the problem and your decisions counts as real proof of work, especially if 70% or more
        of it reflects your own thinking.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="rnl-num">4</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Rebuild your resume around outcomes</strong>, not tasks. Replace vague client-project
        language with specific systems, numbers, and decisions, even directionally estimated ones you can
        defend in an interview.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="rnl-num">5</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Use referrals and LinkedIn outreach deliberately</strong>, not a blind application flood.
        A short, specific message with one observation about the company, a link to your project, and one
        clear question outperforms a generic "please refer me."
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="resume">Fixing the resume so it survives the first filter</h2>

  <p>
    Most service-company resumes get filtered before a human ever reads them carefully, because the
    language describes tasks instead of outcomes.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Weak (task language)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>"Worked on a client project for the banking domain."</li>
        <li>"Responsible for backend development tasks."</li>
        <li>"Handled bug fixes and support tickets."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Stronger (outcome language)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>"Built a payment-reconciliation service processing roughly 10,000 transactions a day."</li>
        <li>"Reduced average API response time by an estimated 30% through query optimisation."</li>
        <li>"Resolved production incidents affecting 500+ users, cutting recurring escalation volume."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you genuinely cannot attach a number to something you did, describe the decision instead: what you
    chose, why, and what changed because of that choice. A specific decision still reads stronger than a
    vague duty.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you start applying broadly</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you whether the switch fits your life. The 3 Gates make you test your
    actual readiness before you spend months applying and interviewing.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not mass-apply to product companies before passing all three gates.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      {gateItems.map((item) => (
        <div class="gate">
          <span class="gate__num">{item.num}</span>
          <span class="gate__name">{item.title}</span>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are unsure whether your specific profile is close to ready, a session inside
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a> can help you compare your actual readiness against a real plan, instead of guessing alone
    from forum threads and conflicting advice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="non-coding">If you are not a pure developer: testers, support, and business analysts</h2>

  <p>
    This switch is not only a developer's story. Service companies employ far more testers, support
    engineers, and business analysts than pure developers, and each of these roles has a real, mapped path
    into a product company or GCC.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {nonCodingItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    The pattern is the same across all three: name the production-reality experience you already have,
    then package one specific, documented example of it instead of leaning on a generic job title.
  </p>

